# Use case backlog

Use cases a split left for later, in dependency order. Each row carries a **`BL-NN`**, its own
identifier, assigned when the row is appended and never reused. **It is not a `UC` number and
never becomes one** — the two sequences are independent, which is what lets a row be dropped or
merged without leaving a hole in the `UC` sequence. No slug either: both the number and the slug
are fixed only when the case is designed.

`BL-NN` exists so that a spec can point at a row. An impact row that adds a precondition names
the case that satisfies it, and when that case is still in the backlog there was previously
nothing to name — two reports invented `UC-004`, a number that existed nowhere, because the
sentence needed one.

| Id | Depends on | Description for `/new-feature` | Split from |
|---|---|---|---|
| `BL-01` | `UC-001-create-customer` | REST endpoint that reads one customer by id from the `customers` table. Not found is a business error | `UC-001-create-customer` |
| `BL-02` | `UC-001-create-customer` | REST endpoint that renames a customer in the `customers` table. Name must stay unique | `UC-001-create-customer` |
| `BL-03` | `UC-001-create-customer` | REST endpoint that deletes a customer by id from the `customers` table | `UC-001-create-customer` |

## Retired

A row leaves the table above when its `/new-feature` starts and the real `UC-NNN` is assigned.
It lands here instead of being deleted: a spec that cited `BL-NN` is immutable once approved, so
the citation has to stay resolvable exactly at the moment the case stops being backlog. This is
also the only place that records which backlog row a use case came from.

| Id | Became | Date |
|---|---|---|
| `BL-00` | `UC-002-create-account` | 2026-01-15 |

